<section class="grading-instructions">
    <h1>Grading Instructions</h4>
    <p>I recommend duplicating this browser tab when grading. I went a little overboard with the features, but I think they're all useful and well-integrated.</p>
    <div class="added-features">
        <h3>Features Added</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Account Management System</li>
            <li>Custom Vehicle Orders</li>
            <li>Admin Management Tools</li>
            <li>Dynamic color displays - dynamic for each vehicle and custom order.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>All features can be viewed and managed through the "My Account" view.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="login-instructions">
        <h3>Login Instructions</h3>
        <p>Log in using the credentials provided in the assignment comment. They are also the default for the course materials</p>
        <p>See the level of access for each account by logging in to each account type:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>All accounts have access to view inventory and place custom orders.</li>
            <li>Customer: Can view and update their own account information, view inventory, and place custom orders.</li>
            <li>Employee: All customer permissions plus the ability to view all custom orders.</li>
            <li>Admin: All employee permissions plus the ability to manage user accounts and update order statuses.</li>
            <li>Admin accounts have safegaurds for ensuring that there is always at least one admin account in the system.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="order-creation">
        <h3>Custom Order Creation Instructions</h3>
        <p>To create a custom order, log in to a customer account and navigate to the "Build Custom Vehicle" page. Fill out the form with your desired specifications for the custom vehicle, including year, make, model, color, and any additional features or notes. Once you submit the form, your custom order will be created and you can view it in the "View My Orders" section of your account.</p>
        <p>Employee accounts can view all custom orders in the "Admin Orders" section of the site.</p>
        <p>You may notice that each account can view their own custom orders in the "View My Orders" section of their account. This is separate from the "Manage Custom Orders" section available to Admin accounts.</p>
        <p>Admin accounts can manage all custom orders, including updating the status and adding notes, in the "Manage Custom Orders" section of the site.</p>
        <p class="p-important">For grading, I recommend creating a custom order with the client account, viewing it with the employee account, and then approving and eventually deleting it with the admin account after checking the status from the client perspective.</p> 
        <p>The order status cannot be anything other than "Pending" if deletion is attempted. I figure this allows for eventually creating a system for "cancelled" orders that still need records; rather than deleting them outright.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="author-note">
        <h3>Author's Note:</h3>
        <p>Thank you for reviewing my work.</p>
        <p>I had a lot of fun building this and learned a lot. As a disclaimer, I did make use of AI to debug and offer solutions to questions and design choices. I hope that this project demonstrates my ability to build a complex, feature-rich web application with attention to detail and user experience. I hope that my understanding of MVC principles and web development best practices is evident in this project, regardless of any outside assistance.</p>
        <p>My only regret is not redesigning the front page to be more active and dynamic.</p>
        <p>Also, I kind of hate the design expectations that were expected from the assignment instructions. I feel the checkerboard and other design choices could be more refined but I of all people understand that sometimes the customer is king and you can't do it all.</p>
